  <!-- 2. Editorial Body Section -->
  <section class="fm-art-body-sec">
    <article class="fm-art-body-inner rte">
      {%- if article.content != blank -%}
        {{ article.content }}
      {%- else -%}
        {%- case a_key -%}
          {%- when 'hand-tufted' -%}
            <p>Hand-tufting is one of the most versatile, characterful, and painterly rug-making techniques in the world. Unlike hand-knotting — which is beautiful but rigidly geometric — hand-tufting allows artisans to work with brush-like freedom, sculpting depth, color and form with remarkable precision.</p>

            <h2>How a Farsh Mahal rug is made</h2>
            <p>Each rug begins with a full-scale drawing on cotton canvas stretched across a wooden frame. Skilled artisans use a hand-held tufting gun to punch wool yarn through the canvas, following the design line by line, color by color.</p>
            <p>Once the tufting is complete, a layer of latex is applied to the back to hold the tufts in place. A cotton backing is then affixed by hand, and the pile is sheared, carved, and finished — sometimes in high-low sculpted textures, sometimes flat and even.</p>

            <blockquote class="fm-art-quote">
              <p>“Between 120 and 220 hours of hand craftsmanship go into each Farsh Mahal rug.”</p>
            </blockquote>

            <h2>Why it matters</h2>
            <p>Hand-tufting gives rugs a richness and depth that machine-made pieces can't replicate. It also allows for a much broader range of design — from painterly abstract landscapes to crisp modernist grids.</p>

          {%- when 'layering' -%}
            <p>Rug layering is one of the interior design world's best-kept secrets — a way to add depth, warmth, and personality to a room without buying new furniture.</p>

            <h2>The basics</h2>
            <p>The rule of thumb: pair a larger, neutral base rug (usually a flat or low-pile natural fiber like jute or wool) with a smaller, more decorative rug placed on top and off-center.</p>

            <blockquote class="fm-art-quote">
              <p>“Layering rugs is like layering clothing — it's about texture, proportion, and letting each piece do its own job.”</p>
            </blockquote>

            <h2>Where it works best</h2>
            <p>Layering shines in living rooms and bedrooms where you want to define a specific zone — a reading corner, a conversation area, or the space at the foot of the bed. It's also brilliant over wall-to-wall carpet in older homes.</p>

          {%- when 'color-trends' -%}
            <p>The most enduring interior color palettes share one quality: they feel inevitable. They're not trendy, they don't shout, and they age beautifully. Here are the palettes we're drawn to in 2026.</p>

            <h2>Warm neutrals with green</h2>
            <p>Cream, oat, and warm putty paired with a single deep-forest accent — like our own Farsh Green — feel both timeless and quietly modern.</p>

            <h2>Sun-worn earths</h2>
            <p>Terracotta, ochre, and softened rust, grounded by aged brass and dark walnut, evoke a Mediterranean serenity that reads beautifully in American homes.</p>

          {%- when 'texture' -%}
            <p>In a photograph, pattern is what catches the eye. In a room, it's texture that keeps you there.</p>

            <h2>Depth without noise</h2>
            <p>A sculpted, hi-lo hand-tufted rug adds an extraordinary amount of visual and tactile depth to a room without introducing a single new color. It's why textural rugs are our most-recommended piece for calm, quiet spaces.</p>

          {%- else -%}
            <p>The right rug doesn't merely fill a space — it defines it. Choosing the correct size is one of the most important design decisions you'll make for a room, and one that too often gets overlooked. Below, our design team walks you through the guidelines we use with clients across the country.</p>

            <h2>Living rooms</h2>
            <p>In the living room, aim for a rug large enough to sit under the front legs of all seating pieces at a minimum. For a more grounded, generous feel, choose a rug that fits all four legs of every piece — this is our preferred approach in most American homes.</p>

            <blockquote class="fm-art-quote">
              <p>“A rug that's too small floats. A rug that's the right size grounds the entire room and makes it feel considered.”</p>
            </blockquote>

            <h2>Dining rooms</h2>
            <p>Under a dining table, allow at least 24 inches of rug to extend past each edge. This ensures the back legs of chairs remain on the rug even when pulled out — a small detail that changes how the room feels every day.</p>

            <h2>Bedrooms</h2>
            <p>For a king or queen bed, we love the softness of a 9 × 12 or 10 × 14 rug centered under the bed, with 2–3 feet extending on either side. That first warm step in the morning is worth the investment.</p>

            <h2>Halls and entryways</h2>
            <p>Runners work beautifully in hallways longer than six feet, leaving 4–6 inches of floor visible on each side. Round rugs are unexpectedly wonderful in circular foyers or beneath round tables — they soften the geometry of a room instantly.</p>
            <p>When in doubt, size up. A slightly larger rug will almost always feel more considered than one that sits awkwardly small in the middle of the room.</p>
        {%- endcase -%}
      {%- endif -%}
    </article>
  </section>

